# Private paper route

## 1. Scientific objective and author claim

The paper tests whether attaching two neutral boron Lewis-acid ligands B(C6F5)3 to the 41-valence-electron PAl12 superalkali raises the isolated-cluster adiabatic electron affinity (AEA) into the superhalogen regime while retaining the PAl12 superatomic framework. The reported endpoint is the neutral/anionic pair PAl12[B(C6F5)3]2.

## 2. System and model boundary

The isolated gas-phase cluster contains one central P atom, twelve Al atoms, and two B(C6F5)3 ligands (each ligand is B bonded to three C6F5 groups). Neutral charge and one-electron-reduced charge −1 are compared. The AEA is the energy difference between optimized neutral and optimized anion, including zero-point corrections. No graphene, solvent, counterion, or thermal free-energy contribution is part of this endpoint.

## 3. Authors' implemented computational route

| Step | Purpose | Input | Method/software | Key parameters | Output | Source evidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Optimize candidate neutral ground state | PAl12[B(C6F5)3]2, several ligand sites and spin multiplicities | DFT, Gaussian 16 | PBE0/def2-SVP; compare possible multiplicities and binding sites | lowest-energy neutral geometry and energy | ev_doc_4f9490507c88_000002_2257e7560ecc; ev_doc_6507cac94f8a_000012_87fcd515b5ec |
| 2 | Verify neutral minimum | optimized neutral candidates | harmonic frequencies, same level | absence of imaginary frequencies; ZPE | validated neutral energy | ev_doc_4f9490507c88_000002_2257e7560ecc |
| 3 | Optimize candidate anion ground state | PAl12[B(C6F5)3]2−, corresponding sites and multiplicities | DFT, Gaussian 16 | PBE0/def2-SVP | lowest-energy anion geometry and energy | ev_doc_4f9490507c88_000002_2257e7560ecc; ev_doc_4f9490507c88_000006_3500ac5b9a0e |
| 4 | Verify anion minimum | optimized anion candidates | harmonic frequencies, same level | absence of imaginary frequencies; ZPE | validated anion energy | ev_doc_4f9490507c88_000002_2257e7560ecc |
| 5 | Compute AEA | validated neutral and anion | energy difference with ZPE | E(neutral)−E(anion), converted Hartree to eV | AEA | ev_doc_6507cac94f8a_000012_87fcd515b5ec |
| 6 | Interpret electronic effect | validated structures | frontier-MO, Hirshfeld/ESP and superatomic-state analyses | compare ligand-free and ligated systems | preserved framework and downward-shift interpretation | ev_doc_6507cac94f8a_000002_2257e7560ecc; ev_doc_6507cac94f8a_000027_3943e25f7727 |

## 4. Validation and analysis protocol

The authors examined alternative ligand binding positions and spin multiplicities, selected the lowest-energy structures, and required frequency minima. They included zero-point corrections. Method validation used calculated Al13 AEA (~3.54 eV) versus experiment (3.57 ± 0.05 eV) and PAl12 AIP (~5.25 eV) versus experiment (5.37 ± 0.04 eV). For PAl12[B(C6F5)3]n, they report preservation of the icosahedral metal-core/superatomic framework, ligand-induced charge transfer, and 300 K/10 ps AIMD stability.

## 5. Private reference results

The main-paper reference AEA for PAl12[B(C6F5)3]2 is 3.93 eV; the corresponding bare PAl12 AEA is 1.76 eV and the one-ligand value is 3.22 eV. The paper reports that the two-ligand P system favors a para-position arrangement, that superatomic shell occupancy is retained, and that the ligand lowers the frontier spectrum. These values and interpretations are evaluator-only.

## 6. Limitations and interpretation boundaries

The source does not provide Cartesian coordinate tables or explicit ground-state multiplicities. A fair task therefore requires independent construction of chemically valid starting geometries and an explicit multiplicity/site search, while scoring the converged, frequency-validated endpoint rather than a paper drawing. Agreement is method- and model-dependent; the isolated-cluster AEA must not be interpreted as a condensed-phase or experimental observable without qualification.
